# Stardrive operator configuration.
#
# Copy to `prod.env` at the repo root and fill in the blanks:
#
#     node services/api/scripts/make-prod-env.mjs
#
# That script copies this file and generates the two values you cannot look up
# anywhere, because nobody issues them. `prod.env` is gitignored; this file is
# not, so NEVER put a real value in here.
#
# Everything is dormant until its variable is set. A missing key never breaks
# the service, it switches one capability off and says so honestly.

# ── Required ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generated for you. Encrypts every licensee's hosting tokens and every
# per-site key, so it must be the SAME string on every restart and every
# redeploy, forever.
#
# Put it in your password manager the moment it exists, kept apart from any
# backup of the data. Restore the data without it and you get accounts and
# sites whose credentials are all dead. There is a drill for exactly this in
# services/api/test/backup-restore.mjs.
#
# If you leave this unset the server writes var/secret.key on first boot,
# which is right for development and wrong for production: the volume then
# holds both the ciphertext and the key to it.
STARDRIVE_SECRET=

# Your real public address, scheme included, no trailing slash. This is the
# origin used in every link that LEAVES the server: email verification,
# password resets, and the intake link you hand to a client.
#
# Without it those links are built from the request's Host header, which is
# always http:// behind a TLS-terminating proxy (so a one-shot auth token
# travels in plaintext on the first hop) and is attacker-controlled (so a
# stranger can trigger a reset for someone else and point the emailed link at
# a host they own). Set it the moment you have a domain.
STARDRIVE_PUBLIC_URL=https://stardrive.dev

# ── The model, which turns the Studio on ─────────────────────────────────
# platform.openai.com -> API keys -> create secret key. Starts sk-, shown once.
# The account needs credit: a key with no balance is not a dormant Studio, it
# is a 502 on every generation.
#
# One key covers template generation, the site copywriter, and Batch Building.
# Customers never bring a model key; this is yours.
STARDRIVE_LLM_KEY=

# Defaults are the operator decision of 23 July 2026 and need no entry:
#   STARDRIVE_LLM_PROVIDER   openai (the alternative is anthropic)
#   STARDRIVE_LLM_MODEL      gpt-5.6-sol
#   STARDRIVE_COPY_MODEL     gpt-5.5, deliberately lighter than the Studio's
# Uncomment to switch providers. An Anthropic key comes from
# console.anthropic.com and the model default becomes claude-sonnet-5.
#STARDRIVE_LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic

# ── Your own view of the service ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Generated for you. Unlocks GET /v1/ops: uptime, error counts, free disk, the
# oldest running build, the last 50 errors. Nothing is encrypted with it, so
# losing it costs nothing and you can rotate it whenever you like.
#
# Deliberately not an API-key scope. No licensee should ever read the queue,
# the disk, or another tenant's error paths.
STARDRIVE_OPS_TOKEN=

# ── Email ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# resend.com -> API keys. This is STARDRIVE'S OWN key, for signup welcomes,
# email verification, password resets, access requests and watchdog alerts.
#
# NOT the same slot as a client site's Resend key. Those are set per site in
# the Workbench, under Site settings, and belong to whoever owns that client's
# hosting. Never put a client's key here.
#
# ORDERING MATTERS: setting this is what switches email verification on.
# Accounts created before it exists are treated as verified forever; accounts
# created after must confirm. Set it before you open signup, not after, or you
# will run two cohorts that behave differently.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# The sending identity. Its domain must be verified in Resend (Domains, add
# the domain, set the DNS records) or every send fails.
STARDRIVE_EMAIL_FROM=Stardrive <onboarding@stardrive.dev>

# Where access requests land, and where the watchdog writes when something
# breaks. ALERT_TO falls back to LEADS_TO when unset.
STARDRIVE_LEADS_TO=
#STARDRIVE_ALERT_TO=

# ── Billing, when you are ready to charge ────────────────────────────────
# dashboard.stripe.com. The checkout and plan-flip code is built and tested;
# it activates on these. Point a Stripe webhook at POST /webhooks/stripe.
#STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
#STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#STRIPE_PRICE_STARTER=
#STRIPE_PRICE_STUDIO=
#STRIPE_PRICE_AGENCY=

# ── Already set by the Dockerfile, listed so you know they are handled ───
#   NODE_ENV=production
#   PORT=8080
#   STARDRIVE_ENGINE=real          real assembly and export
#   STARDRIVE_QA=full              install -> next build -> serve -> routes -> axe
#   STARDRIVE_VAR_DIR=/data        mount a volume here; it IS the business
#   STARDRIVE_SECURE_COOKIES=1     you are behind HTTPS

# ── Tuning. Defaults are sane; touch these only with a reason ────────────
#   RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN            120    per key
#   SIGNUP_LIMIT_PER_HOUR         5      accounts one IP may create
#   STARDRIVE_BUILD_CONCURRENCY   2      a full-QA build is CPU and RAM hungry
#   STARDRIVE_QUEUE_ALERT         25     queued builds worth an email
#   STARDRIVE_MIN_FREE_MB         disk floor below which builds are refused
#   STARDRIVE_LLM_MAX_TURNS       40
#   STARDRIVE_LLM_MAX_INPUT_CHARS 300000
#   STARDRIVE_LLM_MAX_TOKENS      48000
#   STARDRIVE_PLAYWRIGHT          path to an install, for the browser QA checks
#   STARDRIVE_AXE                 path to axe-core, for the accessibility check
